     1) Disabling the "quit" feature wouldn't get much done, IMHO.  It's
too easy to just let some grid bug zap you to death.
     Besides, I don't like the idea of playing with system features of
a game to affect the play balance.  If the "quit" option is disabled,
(perhaps it should just utterly wipe your character out of existence)
people will just ^C out, most likely, for whatever reason, which isn't
a particularly clean way of handling things.
     2) Essentially, I don't like the idea (just a gut instinct) of
destroying items in a poly-pile.  That can be defeated (as someone
pointed out) with a big enough pile, *or* it will be just too difficult
to use polymorph at all with a small pile (in a non-overbalancing way).
I think just dealing with the top item of a pile, or a maximum of n
items (5-10, most likely, with a ragged end) seems like the best
approach.
     (Better yet, polymorph five random items within the pile so that
players trying to hold a "poly-party" end up hunting through the pile
for bits...)
     I suppose, in the spirit of things, we could shorten the game and
just make the objective to zap a wand of polymorph at 30 items or more :).
     3) "If you don't like it, don't use it?"  That's rediculous; it's no
excuse for overbalancing the game, and making winning it a matter of
patience rather than skill (sure; you can win it without poly-piling, but
you'll probably be the only one on the scoreboard who has).


